{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785443136,"nonce":"JCB6LPkUSUlryEnM3C95AVc4w8Q=","title":"The canonicalisation-skew tripwire in TieredCas is unreachable in production, and its comment claims a protection nobody has","message":"Established during ADR-0064 s1 review; recorded in `crates/scarab-storage/src/tiered.rs`'s module docs by that slice, with no code change.\n\n`put_tree` compares `warm_hash` against `hash` and reports disagreement as canonicalisation skew, the stated hazard being a differently-versioned binary computing a different tree hash from the same entries (ADR-0061 s8). The twin in `tree_entries` does the same on the read path.\n\nOn the control-plane instance both sides come from the caller's **own linked** `scarab_storage::canonical_tree` — `crates/scarab-workspace-client/src/lib.rs:331-335` computes the hash client-side before it ever reaches the wire. Two calls to one compiled function cannot differ. The branch is unreachable in production as written, in both directions.\n\nThe only place two genuinely independent walks were ever compared was the hand-rolled double-ingest in `workspaced.rs`, and ADR-0064 s1 deleted it (one walk now, nothing to disagree).\n\nSo the tripwire is currently decoration that reads as protection, which is worse than no tripwire: a reader concludes the hazard is covered.\n\nDecide one:\n- Give it a real second source — have the Depot return the address it filed on `PUT /v1/cas/trees/{hash}` and compare that against the client's, which is a genuine cross-binary comparison and the hazard ADR-0061 s8 actually named.\n- Delete it and say in the module docs that cross-binary canonicalisation skew is undetected, so the next person knows it is open rather than handled.\n\n- [ ] Either the comparison has two independent sources, or the code is gone and the gap is stated\n- [ ] ADR-0061 s8's claim is reconciled with whichever is chosen","files":null}]}